TITLE:: PltObservers
summary:: Several analysers on one sound, read through one another
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Overviews/PlotLib, Classes/PltUmwelt, Classes/PltRecurrence, Classes/PltTrack

DESCRIPTION::
One sound measured by several observers, each a descriptor at an analysis size. Every
track is normalised to its own range and drawn with the others faintly behind it; the
last panel is their spread, frame by frame. Where it rises, the observers disagree
about what the sound is doing, and that disagreement is the information.

Frames quieter than code::floorDb:: are shaded. Every descriptor still reports a
value there, and a centroid of near silence is as confident as any other.

note::
Tracks are normalised between their 2nd and 98th percentiles, so they compare as
shapes. The caption gives the mean correlation over every pair.
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: fromSoundFile
argument:: path
argument:: observers
An Array of Events: code::(descriptor: \centroid, fftSize: 1024)::, optionally with
code::name:: and a code::func:: of code::{ |frame, sampleRate, fftSize, prev| }:: in
place of a descriptor. Defaults to code::defaultObservers::.
argument:: hop
Fraction of a window between frames.
argument:: points
Width of the shared time grid.
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: sweep
One descriptor at several FFT sizes. The coarse observers keep their steps, so what
a window length decides is visible.
argument:: path
argument:: descriptor
argument:: sizes
argument:: hop
argument:: points
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: new
Any analysers, as an Array of Arrays of values each spread evenly over
code::duration::. A pitch track from another program compares this way.
argument:: values
argument:: duration
argument:: names
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: describe
One number from one frame of magnitudes: code::\centroid::, code::\spread::,
code::\rolloff:: (85% of the power), code::\flatness:: (of the power, as
link::Classes/SpecFlatness::), code::\peak:: (the loudest bin above DC),
code::\flux:: (increases since code::prev::) or code::\level:: (dB of the loudest
bin). A silent frame gives 0.

METHOD:: analyse
The data behind code::fromSoundFile::, without a window.
METHOD:: defaultObservers
METHOD:: grid
METHOD:: frameTimes
Frame centres in seconds.
METHOD:: resample
Mean of the frames in each grid cell, or the nearest frame where a cell has none, so
a coarse observer stays stepped.
METHOD:: normalise
METHOD:: percentile
METHOD:: correlation
METHOD:: agreement
Mean correlation over every pair of tracks.
METHOD:: runs
A Boolean Array as code::[start, end)::  pairs.

INSTANCEMETHODS::
METHOD:: tracks
The normalised tracks.
METHOD:: spread
METHOD:: silent
METHOD:: agreement
METHOD:: names
METHOD:: details
METHOD:: times
METHOD:: duration
METHOD:: floorDb
METHOD:: panelGap
METHOD:: colorKeys

EXAMPLES::
code::
p = Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds/a11wlk01.wav";

// four ideas of brightness
PltObservers.fromSoundFile(p).front;

// one descriptor, four window lengths
PltObservers.sweep(p, \centroid, [256, 1024, 4096, 16384]).front;

// your own observers
(
PltObservers.fromSoundFile(p, [
	(descriptor: \flux, fftSize: 512),
	(descriptor: \flux, fftSize: 4096),
	(name: "high band", fftSize: 1024, func: { |frame| frame.copyToEnd(200).sum })
]).front;
)

// tracks from anywhere, here three made-up ones
PltObservers([{ 1.0.rand } ! 100, (0..99).sin, (0..49)], 5, ["noise", "sine", "ramp"]).front;
::
